    "title": "The Exact Phantom Number of the Cofinite Topology",
    "description": "For an infinite set X, the cofinite topology (opens are the empty set and the complements of finite sets) was shown to be the consensus (open-set intersection) of exactly two strictly finer topologies, each obtained by additionally declaring one distinguished singleton open. Conjecture: the phantom number of the cofinite topology is exactly 2, i.e. it is not 'meet-irreducible' (it does split into two genuinely finer topologies) but it is also not the trivial one-observer case. Concretely, prove (a) there is no representation of the cofinite topology as a single strictly-finer observer, and (b) characterize all two-observer representations, showing they correspond to choices of two disjoint nonempty 'phantom' open families whose only common member is the empty set. Approach: work in the lattice of topologies ordered by fineness, use that a set open in the consensus is open in every observer, and analyze which pairs of proper coarsenings meet back to cofinite. The key insight is that consensus is a meet of open-set families, so two observers cancel down to the cofinite opens precisely when their extra visible sets intersect only in the empty set. Why now? A concrete infinite T1 non-metrizable space is now known to have phantom number 2, so pinning the number exactly and classifying the witnesses is the natural next quantitative step.",
