    "title": "Consensus-Possibility Gap and a Quantitative 'Observer Effect'",
    "description": "Given a family of topologies (observers) on a set X, its consensus is the coarsest reading (a set is open iff open for every observer) and its possibility is the finest reading (the infimum in the fineness order, generated by all opens visible to some observer). For the two singleton-observers on an infinite set these are two explicit and distinct topologies: the cofinite topology and the cofinite topology enriched by two extra open points. Conjecture: for any finite family of observers, the possibility topology equals the consensus topology if and only if all observers coincide, and otherwise the possibility is strictly finer; moreover the 'size' of the gap (measured by the collection of sets open in the possibility but not the consensus) is exactly the join of the observers' private open families. Approach: use that possibility is a join of open-set families and consensus is a meet, then show equality of join and meet forces all families equal. The key insight is that the difference between the two extremal readings is a precise, computable measure of how much observation adds beyond agreement. Why now? Both extremal readings have just been computed in closed form for a natural family, so abstracting the gap into a general invariant is the immediate generalization.",
